|~ `::construct`, the step that puts a value under a tag through the
   tag's kind, `::T[…]`, and what is declared under it: its
   constructor and the refusals of that step.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:construct
  |~~ The step that puts a payload under a tag, `::T[…]`, `::T{…}`,
      `::T"…"`, or a number, a boolean, null or a path by `#`, `::T#5`,
      through the tag's kind, which attaches the tag or refuses the
      payload.

      In a quote the step is a record, `~(::Box[1 2]) | first` answering
      `::construct{:tag ::Box :payload [1 2]}`, a tag written before an
      error literal among them, `~(::Late!{:k 1}) | first` answering
      `::construct{:tag ::Late :payload !{:k 1}}`: `:tag` the tag and
      `:payload` the step of the payload the literal writes, the record
      built into its step by the constructor of this kind, the second part
      of its literal, `::construct | binding | get /value | get /1`.
      ::link~(::tagged | doc) ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps
        |~~ The record of the step, `:tag` and `:payload`, built into its
            step.
        ~~|
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::construct` builds the step of a tagged literal from its
            record, a map; a payload of another kind is refused, a map
            under another tag among them.
            ::link~(::construct | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :PayloadNotSchemaError
        |~~ A `::construct` is a map of `:tag`, a tag name, and
            `:payload`, a literal, both given, and `:field` names the field
            that does not fit: the tag is written with its two colons, so
            the step `::Box[1]` is
            `{:tag ::Box :payload [1]} | tag ::construct`, and the step
            `::Box!{:k 1}` is `{:tag ::Box :payload !{:k 1}} | tag ::construct`,
            while the kind of errors over an error literal is the literal
            itself, `!{:k 1}`.
            ::link~(::construct | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :LiteralComputesError
        |~~ A payload computes nothing, as every part of a literal: a name
            or a group in it is refused, and `:message` writes the template
            `get` builds the value from, its parts computed written as
            quotes, `get ::Box[~(x)]`, while a value computed goes under a
            tag by `tag`, `x | tag ::Box`.
            ::link~(::construct | doc) ::link~(::any/get | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :LiteralComputesError/message
        |~~ What the rule says of the literal, the template `get` builds it
            from.
        ~~|
        ::string
    | throws :TagNamesAValueError
        |~~ The tag before a payload addresses a kind, a label declared by
            its chain or by its page alone, a verb, whose args it lays
            itself over, `::vec/take[2]`, an invariant, whose refusal it
            lays itself over, or no declaration at all; a tag that addresses
            a name holding a value of its own names nothing to build by, and
            `:tag` is that tag. The value is read by its name, `limit`, and
            is put under a tag of its own by `tag`, `limit | tag ::Limit`.
            ::link~(::construct | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :TagNamesAValueError/tag
        |~~ The tag, whose node is neither a label, a verb nor an
            invariant.
        ~~|
        ::tag
    | proves :holdsTheTagAndThePayload
        |~~ The step holds its tag and its payload. ~~|
        ~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | get [/tag /payload] | eq [::Box [1 2]])
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesATagThatIsAKeyword
        |~~ A `:tag` that is a keyword is refused, `:field` naming it. ~~|
        ~(::construct{:tag :Box :payload [1]}
          !| get [~(type) /field] | eq [::construct/PayloadNotSchemaError :tag])
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesARecordWithoutAPayload
        |~~ A record without its payload is refused, `:field` naming it. ~~|
        ~({:tag ::Box} | tag ::construct
          !| get [~(type) /field] | eq [::construct/PayloadNotSchemaError :payload])
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/buildsTheStepOfATag
        |~~ A tag name and the step of a payload build the step. ~~|
        ~({:tag ::Box :payload [1]} | tag ::construct | get [~(it)] | tag ::quote | eq ~(::Box[1]))
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/buildsTheStepOfAnErrorLiteralUnderATag
        |~~ A tag name and an error literal build the step of the error
            literal written with the tag before its bang.
        ~~|
        ~(~(!{:k 1}) | get {:tag ::Foo :payload /0} | tag ::construct | get [~(it)] | tag ::quote | eq ~(::Foo!{:k 1}))
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesTheKindOfErrorsOverAnErrorLiteral
        |~~ The kind of errors over an error literal is the literal itself,
            and `:field` names the tag.
        ~~|
        ~(::construct{:tag ::error :payload !{:k 1}} !| get [~(type) /field] | eq [::construct/PayloadNotSchemaError :tag])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector is no record of a tagged literal. ~~|
        ~(::construct[1] !| get [~(type) /expected] | eq [::construct/PayloadKindNotTakenError #[::map]])
    | proves :LiteralComputesError/refusesAPayloadOfCode
        |~~ The step of a call as the payload computes, and is refused. ~~|
        ~(get {:tag ::Box :payload ~(~(x) | first)} | tag ::construct
          !| get [~(type) /message] | eq [::construct/LiteralComputesError "a literal computes nothing: a shape computed is built by `get`, its parts paths and quotes, write `get ::Box~(x)`"])
    | proves :writesANumberPayloadByAHash
        |~~ A number payload touches its tag by `#`. ~~|
        ~(::construct{:tag ::Box :payload 42} | get [~(it)] | tag ::quote | eq ~(::Box#42))
    | proves :TagNamesAValueError/refusesTheTagOfAValue
        |~~ The tag of a name that holds a value builds nothing. ~~|
        ~(:limit 5 | ::limit[1]
          !| get [~(type) /tag /actualType] | eq [::construct/TagNamesAValueError ::limit ::number])
    | proves :TagNamesAValueError/laysAVerbsTagOverItsArgs
        |~~ The tag of a verb lays itself over its args. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take[2] | type | eq ::vec/take)
    | proves :TagNamesAValueError/laysAnInvariantsTagOverAValue
        |~~ The tag of an invariant lays itself over a value. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take/count/NotIntegerError#5 | type | eq ::vec/take/count/NotIntegerError)
    | proves :TagNamesAValueError/putsTheValueUnderATagOfItsOwn
        |~~ The value of a name is put under a tag of its own. ~~|
        ~(:limit 5 | limit | tag ::Limit | payload | eq 5)
    | impl ::builtin{}

